SQL Lesson 8: Same SQL, different dialects 🏨

After nearly a decade in hotel revenue and commercial operations, I’ve learned one thing: a query that works on one platform can break on another.

This series uses Snowflake and PostgreSQL. 90% is the same. The 10% that isn’t will catch you out.

Pull the top 10 reservations by revenue:

SELECT reservation_id, hotel_name, room_revenue
FROM reservations
ORDER BY room_revenue DESC
LIMIT 10;

Works in both. TOP 10 is SQL Server only.

Key differences to know:
→ Unquoted names: Snowflake stores them UPPERCASE, PostgreSQL lowercase
→ Both support DATE_TRUNC, LIMIT, ILIKE
→ Snowflake has QUALIFY; PostgreSQL doesn’t

💡 Avoid double quotes around column names unless you have to. Quoted names become case-sensitive.

⚠️ A RevPAR report built on one platform and copied to another can fail silently or return nothing because of a case mismatch.

